WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:01.070
welcome back to the David Pakman show.

00:00:01.070 --> 00:00:04.590
It's great to welcome to the program today,
cal Newport, who is a computer science professor

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:06.660
at Georgetown Town University.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:12.429
Also author of several books including most
recently digital minimalism, choosing a focus

00:00:12.429 --> 00:00:18.220
life in a noisy world previously deep work,
which I recently recommended to my audience

00:00:18.220 --> 00:00:19.220
a cal.

00:00:19.220 --> 00:00:20.220
It's so great to have you on.

00:00:20.220 --> 00:00:21.220
I appreciate it.

00:00:21.220 --> 00:00:22.220
No, thanks for having me.

00:00:22.220 --> 00:00:23.220
It's my pleasure.

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:29.279
So I've been following with great interest,
the sort of societal and cultural impact of

00:00:29.279 --> 00:00:34.530
uh, modern media, social media technology
that includes smartphones and Netflix and

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:38.120
all sorts of a, a sort of on demand entertainment.

00:00:38.120 --> 00:00:41.820
And you've also been writing extensively about
this for a long time.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:46.030
Uh, I don't think it'll come as a shock to
my audience that I agree with a lot of your

00:00:46.030 --> 00:00:50.340
conclusions, many of which we'll talk about
momentarily, but when did you first sort of

00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:57.730
get tipped off to the idea that there is something
going on here that is impacting how people

00:00:57.730 --> 00:01:02.780
sort of go about their days and get work done
or don't get work done or relate to other

00:01:02.780 --> 00:01:03.780
people in the world?

00:01:03.780 --> 00:01:06.530
Like how early on did you pick up on this
issue?

00:01:06.530 --> 00:01:14.000
Well back in 2012 I had published a career
advice book called so good they can't ignore

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:15.000
you.

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.590
And one of the ideas in that book is that
getting really good at rare and valuable skills

00:01:19.590 --> 00:01:22.750
was a huge piece of leverage and craft and
a career that was really meaningful.

00:01:22.750 --> 00:01:27.610
So after that book came out, my readers were
asking me, okay, well how do I do that?

00:01:27.610 --> 00:01:30.060
How do I become really good at things that
are rare and valuable?

00:01:30.060 --> 00:01:31.890
And then I started looking into that question.

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:33.690
I started coming up with this tension.

00:01:33.690 --> 00:01:38.579
I would find something that important, such
as concentration, learning, new skills, producing

00:01:38.579 --> 00:01:39.579
at a high level.

00:01:39.579 --> 00:01:42.430
And every time I come, couple of something
important, I would find this technological

00:01:42.430 --> 00:01:46.939
foil that was unintentionally making it harder
to do that.

00:01:46.939 --> 00:01:52.570
And so my 2016 book deep work was really about
this tension between how concentrating is

00:01:52.570 --> 00:01:55.070
becoming more important in our economy.

00:01:55.070 --> 00:02:00.500
At the same time that technology in the workplace
was making us worse a concentrating.

00:02:00.500 --> 00:02:03.390
And so that book came out and then my readers
pushed back again and said, yeah, but what

00:02:03.390 --> 00:02:05.570
about life outside of work?

00:02:05.570 --> 00:02:09.880
We're feeling like technology, especially
what's happening on our smartphones is taking

00:02:09.880 --> 00:02:13.770
us away or reducing the quality of what we're
trying to do with our life outside of the

00:02:13.770 --> 00:02:14.770
professional context.

00:02:14.770 --> 00:02:17.260
So again, that pushed me into that for digital
minimalism.

00:02:17.260 --> 00:02:23.840
And so this has been the story of my writing
life, let's say over the last seven years

00:02:23.840 --> 00:02:31.080
or so, is keep coming up against these tensions
between human striving to flourish and unintentional

00:02:31.080 --> 00:02:33.890
consequences of technology that are holding
them back from it.

00:02:33.890 --> 00:02:38.360
So let's focus in a little bit first on the
deep work component and then in part two we'll

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:41.940
dig in more deeply into what you outlined
in in digital minimalism.

00:02:41.940 --> 00:02:46.870
I thought before I read your books that I
was handling a lot of these issues really

00:02:46.870 --> 00:02:47.870
pretty well.

00:02:47.870 --> 00:02:51.930
I, I was sort of like satisfied with what
I felt was my work life balance, my relationship

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.980
to technology, the amount of time I was spending
on sort of what, what I would consider to

00:02:55.980 --> 00:03:01.230
be like the core important tasks of for what
I do, which are researching the stories that

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:05.710
make it onto onto my show, preparing and actually
filming the show.

00:03:05.710 --> 00:03:11.010
And when I read deep work, I realized a huge
percentage of what I'm doing is this so called

00:03:11.010 --> 00:03:17.580
shallow work, which is everything from responding
to emails, dealing with administrative tasks,

00:03:17.580 --> 00:03:21.450
uh, and, and sort of all of those things that
aren't really the core of what it is that

00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:28.080
I'm doing for people who, who are not aware
of, of what you're arguing, what percentage

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:33.820
of of many jobs now is consumed, not by sort
of like the core deep work.

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:39.230
But by this shallow work that you identify
as not not sort of core to what one is trying

00:03:39.230 --> 00:03:41.820
to achieve to produce value.

00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:46.890
I think for many knowledge work positions
these days accidentally we've gotten to a

00:03:46.890 --> 00:03:52.880
place where the vast majority of people's
time is being spent, uh, either on non deep

00:03:52.880 --> 00:03:58.230
activities or being done somehow roughly in
parallel with the activities greatly reducing

00:03:58.230 --> 00:03:59.230
their efficacy.

00:03:59.230 --> 00:04:05.270
In other words, I think we're doing a really
poor job right now, allocating cognitive resources

00:04:05.270 --> 00:04:08.070
and an elite knowledge economy like we have
in the US.

00:04:08.070 --> 00:04:12.610
The number one capital resource is attention
capital, the ability of human brains, human

00:04:12.610 --> 00:04:16.380
brains that you employ to actually concentrate
and produce information with new value.

00:04:16.380 --> 00:04:21.570
And we're doing a terrible job right now at
getting the best return on that capital investment.

00:04:21.570 --> 00:04:25.930
And so even just having this terminology,
there's a difference between deep work where

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:29.540
you're trying to actually extract maximum
value from the brain versus shallow work,

00:04:29.540 --> 00:04:31.630
which is how you keep the lights on in the
trains run on time.

00:04:31.630 --> 00:04:34.820
Just understand that there's a difference
between those two, allows you to have these

00:04:34.820 --> 00:04:38.570
types of realizations that wait a second,
we're paying all this money to house all of

00:04:38.570 --> 00:04:41.710
these brains and the put them in Nice offices
and air conditioning and heat them and give

00:04:41.710 --> 00:04:44.170
them benefits so that the brains can be healthy
and produce value.

00:04:44.170 --> 00:04:48.310
And yet we're then allowing these brains to
spend the vast majority of their time doing

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:52.440
sort of non high level non-skilled sort of
administrative style work.

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:56.211
Yeah, I mean it's, I, I'm realizing that,
you know, a lot of this time you're, you're

00:04:56.211 --> 00:05:01.490
doing all of that stuff to sit around and
effectively answer emails and be a go between,

00:05:01.490 --> 00:05:03.950
between other other individuals.

00:05:03.950 --> 00:05:09.280
So at the very top level, obviously we can't,
uh, go, go through everything in an interview

00:05:09.280 --> 00:05:10.280
like this.

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:16.270
But one of the things you talk about is very
clearly delineating when it is that you were

00:05:16.270 --> 00:05:20.590
doing that shallow work and a lot of professions,
you can't get rid of it altogether.

00:05:20.590 --> 00:05:25.380
But it seems that condensing it and compartmentalizing
it into particularly planned and scheduled

00:05:25.380 --> 00:05:29.990
times is is sort of one idea that can be effective
for a lot of people.

00:05:29.990 --> 00:05:35.920
From a very practical standpoint, is that
doable for people in most professions?

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:38.960
I feel like that's one of the most common
things people say when I tell them I'm trying

00:05:38.960 --> 00:05:39.960
to do this.

00:05:39.960 --> 00:05:44.450
They say sounds great if you can do it, but
here's why it would not apply to my particular

00:05:44.450 --> 00:05:45.920
profession, which I'm guessing you hear often.

00:05:45.920 --> 00:05:50.370
I hear it a lot and there's certainly our
professions in which let's say concentrated

00:05:50.370 --> 00:05:54.030
cognitive work, what I call deep work is not
that important, but I also think that people

00:05:54.030 --> 00:05:58.570
vastly overestimate the number of professions
for which that's actually true and what I

00:05:58.570 --> 00:06:01.350
recommend is that people should have clarity.

00:06:01.350 --> 00:06:03.090
This is how much deep work I'm doing.

00:06:03.090 --> 00:06:06.650
This is how much non deep or shallow work
I'm doing and they should have some sense

00:06:06.650 --> 00:06:12.600
of for my position, what is the optimal ratio
in a normal week?

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:16.870
What is the optimal ratio of deep work hours
to shallow work hours from the perspective

00:06:16.870 --> 00:06:21.490
of what optimizes the value I produced for
the organization, I think people should have

00:06:21.490 --> 00:06:22.490
an answer to this question.

00:06:22.490 --> 00:06:26.420
I think people should be discussing this question
with their supervisors or bosses if they work

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:27.420
for someone else.

00:06:27.420 --> 00:06:31.380
I think people should be measuring and seeing
how well they're coming up against this ratio.

00:06:31.380 --> 00:06:33.100
Now the ratio is going to be different for
different people.

00:06:33.100 --> 00:06:34.960
It can be different for different jobs.

00:06:34.960 --> 00:06:39.330
Some jobs, let's say you're a novelist, probably
the ratio should be almost a hundred percent

00:06:39.330 --> 00:06:40.620
deep work at another job.

00:06:40.620 --> 00:06:44.300
Let's say you're a lobbyist here in DC where
I am, well maybe almost all of what you're

00:06:44.300 --> 00:06:47.380
doing is just connecting to people and there's
very little deep work, but you should have

00:06:47.380 --> 00:06:48.380
an answer.

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:52.880
We can't blind ourselves to how much deep
work we're doing versus how much shall on

00:06:52.880 --> 00:06:56.180
what would be better and the feedback I'm
getting from my readers is that once you get

00:06:56.180 --> 00:07:00.260
quantitative on this, this is the ratio that
makes sense for my job.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:01.320
Here's where I am.

00:07:01.320 --> 00:07:02.440
There's a gap.

00:07:02.440 --> 00:07:05.740
Once your once your quantitative on this you
see the stark numbers.

00:07:05.740 --> 00:07:09.830
This sparks a lot more cultural changes than
people assume is possible.

00:07:09.830 --> 00:07:15.500
Now a lot of this also depends for people
who are not in a sort of supervisor business

00:07:15.500 --> 00:07:19.700
owner or entrepreneur role role but rather
are working for somebody else.

00:07:19.700 --> 00:07:23.530
A big part of this is is as you mentioned,
having these conversations with whoever is

00:07:23.530 --> 00:07:29.680
kind of monitoring your time because the expectations
from a sort of modern employers often would

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.800
create a problem for a lot of people.

00:07:31.800 --> 00:07:35.200
If they were saying, hey, you know half the
day I'm going to try to do three hours for

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:39.030
example, or four hours, which you've suggested,
if I recall correctly, maybe sort of the Max

00:07:39.030 --> 00:07:43.530
for this deep work that someone may be able
to get done in a normal day.

00:07:43.530 --> 00:07:48.699
A lot of this has to do with the culture in
which people are working, does it not?

00:07:48.699 --> 00:07:54.560
It does, but we think that these cultures
are entrenched and are immutable, but the

00:07:54.560 --> 00:07:58.780
reality is often that they're arbitrary and
emergent and are much more malleable than

00:07:58.780 --> 00:08:00.740
we think once we actually come at them.

00:08:00.740 --> 00:08:01.740
Precisely.

00:08:01.740 --> 00:08:04.740
So here's a classic story that I heard after
my book came out.

00:08:04.740 --> 00:08:08.710
I was someone who was in marketing for Silicon
Valley firms.

00:08:08.710 --> 00:08:13.810
So he wrote white papers, which are incredibly
complex and really from deep work.

00:08:13.810 --> 00:08:17.590
And so he went to his CEO and said, okay,
here's what deep work is.

00:08:17.590 --> 00:08:19.600
Here's what shallow work is.

00:08:19.600 --> 00:08:21.949
What ratio of deep to shallow work makes the
most sense for my job.

00:08:21.949 --> 00:08:25.750
Now, he clarified before he did this, he was
very nervous because this organization had

00:08:25.750 --> 00:08:30.660
a slack culture in which if you did not respond
to slack right away, you were seen as ironically

00:08:30.660 --> 00:08:31.660
slacking off.

00:08:31.660 --> 00:08:36.200
But he had this conversation with the CEO
and he said it became, uh, almost immediately

00:08:36.200 --> 00:08:39.080
clear that it would be crazy for the answer
to be zero.

00:08:39.080 --> 00:08:42.680
They decided that 50, 50 made sense and it
took about a week for them to change this.

00:08:42.680 --> 00:08:47.230
She just told his team these two hours in
the morning, these two hours in the afternoon,

00:08:47.230 --> 00:08:49.089
that's when he is doing his deep work.

00:08:49.089 --> 00:08:50.981
He's not to be interrupted, don't expect to
response.

00:08:50.981 --> 00:08:55.690
It took people about a week to get used to
it and now suddenly this guy has a 50 50 deep

00:08:55.690 --> 00:08:59.510
shallow ratio with complete buy in from his
team and the CEO and it took them only at

00:08:59.510 --> 00:09:03.670
let's say a week to actually change where
before he had this conversation, he was convinced

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:04.670
this is a slack place.

00:09:04.670 --> 00:09:05.770
This is a slack office.

00:09:05.770 --> 00:09:07.220
If you're not responding, it'll never work.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:11.871
So I think we think these cultures are there
for a reason and they're entrenched and our

00:09:11.871 --> 00:09:13.620
bosses are going to insist on them.

00:09:13.620 --> 00:09:17.500
You push a little deeper and you realize often
nobody is happy with them and a great change

00:09:17.500 --> 00:09:21.370
is possible as long as you're clear, positive
and quantitative about why you're making the

00:09:21.370 --> 00:09:22.370
change.

00:09:22.370 --> 00:09:23.370
Yeah.

00:09:23.370 --> 00:09:27.300
After reading deep work and a bunch of articles
about slack, I immediately canceled slack

00:09:27.300 --> 00:09:30.270
fully and told all of my employees were, were
simply not using it.

00:09:30.270 --> 00:09:31.270
And in fact, I went further.

00:09:31.270 --> 00:09:35.540
I said, hey, you know, I, I'm really going
to be trying to, to spend most of the morning,

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:40.630
uh, on, on what is I think is the most important
thing, which is really researching and preparing

00:09:40.630 --> 00:09:41.630
for the show.

00:09:41.630 --> 00:09:47.030
So I will check my email when I get to my
desk and I will not look at email again for

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:48.040
several hours.

00:09:48.040 --> 00:09:51.830
If there's a real emergency text me, I'm not
going to ignore you.

00:09:51.830 --> 00:09:52.830
Right?

00:09:52.830 --> 00:09:55.310
I mean, these are employees and to some degree
I need to be available.

00:09:55.310 --> 00:09:57.950
But if it's not an emergency, you're not going
to hear back from me.

00:09:57.950 --> 00:10:01.290
And very quickly everybody just does seem
to adjust.

00:10:01.290 --> 00:10:06.800
So a lot of it is, as you're saying, what
you think is necessary versus what, what is

00:10:06.800 --> 00:10:09.779
sort of actually a necessary in many cases.

00:10:09.779 --> 00:10:17.180
And, and along those same lines, I'm curious,
um, is there a way or do you have any anecdotes

00:10:17.180 --> 00:10:24.430
about how we can quantify the effect of these
longer periods of concentration on productivity?

00:10:24.430 --> 00:10:30.880
Because I think one of the biggest misconceptions
is that one gets more done by switching between

00:10:30.880 --> 00:10:37.530
tasks and sort of covering a lot of basis
in a less, in a less deep way, but qualitatively

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:42.450
and quantitatively, it actually seems counter,
uh, uh, to, to, to the reality.

00:10:42.450 --> 00:10:48.160
How can we sort of explain how the focus for
longer periods of time quantitatively actually

00:10:48.160 --> 00:10:50.610
allows people to get more done?

00:10:50.610 --> 00:10:57.410
Well, I think a mindset shift that helps here
is thinking about efforts in terms of uh,

00:10:57.410 --> 00:10:59.740
let's say learn skilled applied.

00:10:59.740 --> 00:11:03.920
So if you're, if you're answering emails or,
or, or jumping on calls or moving things back

00:11:03.920 --> 00:11:07.860
and forth in PowerPoint, these are all activities
that don't actually require the application

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:10.190
of sort of hard one or elite level.

00:11:10.190 --> 00:11:15.029
And so the value they're producing is not
actually that high as compared to, let's say

00:11:15.029 --> 00:11:19.041
you're thinking very deeply on researching
a piece you're going to do for your show is

00:11:19.041 --> 00:11:21.210
something that you're uniquely qualified to
do.

00:11:21.210 --> 00:11:22.970
That builds on a large foundation of experience.

00:11:22.970 --> 00:11:27.710
It requires a lot of concentration that is
going to produce much more value because that's

00:11:27.710 --> 00:11:30.170
much more rare or hard to do.

00:11:30.170 --> 00:11:34.180
And so I have this trick in the book where
I talk about quantify the activity you're

00:11:34.180 --> 00:11:39.740
about to do with how many hours would it take
to train a smart recent college Grad to do

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:40.740
what you're about to do.

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:44.030
And if the answer is I could probably train
them in a few hours to do this, then that's

00:11:44.030 --> 00:11:46.940
probably not an activity that's going to be
massively value producing.

00:11:46.940 --> 00:11:51.170
But if on the other hand it's, well, it probably
would take them four or five years of training

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:54.029
and work experience where they could even
approximate what I'm about to be doing.

00:11:54.029 --> 00:11:57.970
You're getting a much higher return on the
cognitive resource for that activity.

00:11:57.970 --> 00:12:01.000
And so I think this mindset shift is crucial.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:06.730
Shallow work is necessary, uh, to keep things
running, but it doesn't produce a ton of value.

00:12:06.730 --> 00:12:11.210
Deep work is the actual, you know, the wrench
is on the car building the thing that's going

00:12:11.210 --> 00:12:13.450
to go off the assembly line to be sold type
activities.

00:12:13.450 --> 00:12:18.470
Once you have that mindset shift, you begin
to worry about shallow work and how much time

00:12:18.470 --> 00:12:22.100
your employees are spending on it and really
start to focus on on the deep work because

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:23.980
that's what produces the value.

00:12:23.980 --> 00:12:28.410
So in part two we'll dig in much more deeply
to digital minimalism, which is the latest

00:12:28.410 --> 00:12:33.550
book, but to start getting us in that mindset
of and transitioning into that, can you talk

00:12:33.550 --> 00:12:39.540
a little bit about what sort of psychological
problems or emotional, uh, sort of sort of

00:12:39.540 --> 00:12:47.200
issues can arise when we become attached to
this type of quote multitasking, which I know

00:12:47.200 --> 00:12:51.240
we, we, you and I actually take issue with
the idea that it's actually multitasking rather

00:12:51.240 --> 00:12:53.400
than switching between things really quickly.

00:12:53.400 --> 00:12:57.910
What are the issues that can develop longer
term in terms of concentration, in terms of

00:12:57.910 --> 00:12:59.930
emotional issues as well?

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:01.860
Well, there's a few issues.

00:13:01.860 --> 00:13:04.060
One is context switching.

00:13:04.060 --> 00:13:08.970
So switching to an inbox and then back to
a primary task reduces your cognitive capacity

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:11.310
and that effect takes awhile to clear out.

00:13:11.310 --> 00:13:15.820
And so if you are constantly having to jump
over and check an inbox or a slack channel,

00:13:15.820 --> 00:13:17.860
then returned to what you're doing.

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:19.399
You're not really single tasking.

00:13:19.399 --> 00:13:23.620
This context switching is having a huge psychological
burden on, on what you're actually able to

00:13:23.620 --> 00:13:24.620
produce.

00:13:24.620 --> 00:13:28.560
And so it's like buying a fancy piece of equipment
in the, in a factory, but then allowing sand

00:13:28.560 --> 00:13:31.080
to build up in his gear so it can't function
as hard.

00:13:31.080 --> 00:13:32.080
It's a real crisis.

00:13:32.080 --> 00:13:33.370
I don't think we understand it.

00:13:33.370 --> 00:13:38.860
There's also a social drag on a workplace
that's built on constant ongoing conversation.

00:13:38.860 --> 00:13:42.720
Our brains are sophisticated social computers
that evolved in a context where we didn't

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:48.399
have digital inboxes, and so you might know
rationally that those 600 unread emails in

00:13:48.399 --> 00:13:52.670
your inbox are different than let's say a
member of your tribe sitting across from the

00:13:52.670 --> 00:13:54.660
fire trying to get your attention.

00:13:54.660 --> 00:13:57.580
But there's a deep part of your brain that
doesn't see that distinction and it sees it

